      "title": "Use chemical symbols for common elements",
      "explanation": "Symbols are the shorthand names for elements. Each symbol starts with a capital letter; if there is a second letter, it is lower-case. Examples: H, He, Na, Cl, Fe. A symbol names an element, not a word. A molecule such as CO is carbon monoxide, a compound, not an element, so do not treat CO as one element. The capital and lowercase letters also help you read formulas: they show which elements are present and how many atoms there are if there is a subscript. A common mistake is thinking CO is an element; it’s a compound. Practice by pairing element names with their symbols and spotting the lowercase second letter.",
