      "title": "Assess the influence of those ideas on revolution and reform",
      "explanation": "Enlightenment ideas supplied a language of rights, consent and rule of law that helped justify revolutions and reform campaigns. Writers argued that governments should reflect the will of the people and protect universal rights, contributing to declarations, constitutions and calls for constitutional change. Print culture and coffee-house discussion spread these arguments far beyond courts and universities, turning philosophy into political action. Yet progress was uneven: economic conditions, tradition and local politics shaped how reform or revolution occurred. A common misconception is that Enlightenment ideas produced uniform, immediate upheaval; in reality outcomes varied and were contested.",
