      "title": "Explain atmospheric pressure and upthrust",
      "explanation": "Atmospheric pressure comes from the air pressing on surfaces from all directions; it varies with height and weather. At a given height it acts on everything and decreases with altitude. Upthrust, or buoyancy, arises because fluid pressure increases with depth, producing a net upward push on submerged objects. Objects float when upthrust balances weight; they sink when weight exceeds upthrust. A common misconception is that air cannot push objects upward; air pressure contributes to everyday buoyancy, acting on all sides of a submerged object. The tendency to float depends on how the displaced fluid compares in density with the object.",
