      "explanation": "Magnification calculations help you link what you see in an image to the real size of a cell. The key formula is Magnification = image size / real size, and you must keep units consistent (for example, converting mm to µm where needed). To use it, measure the image size of a cell on the micrograph with a ruler, then divide by the known real size. Expect to convert scales, such as 1 mm = 1000 µm, to avoid errors. A common misconception is that a larger image always means a larger real size; in fact, the magnification can be high even if the object is tiny, depending on the image size. Calibration with a stage micrometer ensures accuracy.",
