      "title": "Describe the I-V characteristics of a resistor, a filament lamp and a diode",
      "explanation": "I–V characteristics show how components respond to voltage and current. A resistor is ohmic: V ∝ I, giving a straight line through the origin with constant resistance R = V/I. A filament lamp is non-ohmic; as current rises the filament heats, increasing resistance and bending the I–V curve. A diode conducts mainly in forward bias with low resistance and blocks current in reverse bias, producing a highly asymmetric I–V curve. A common misconception is that all components obey Ohm’s law; only linear resistors do so over a range of conditions. Recognise each device by its distinctive I–V shape.",
