      "title": "Use the pH scale and compare acidic solutions",
      "explanation": "The pH scale compares how acidic or alkaline aqueous solutions are. In the usual school scale, acidic solutions have pH below seven, neutral solutions are about pH seven at room temperature, and alkaline solutions have pH above seven. Universal indicator gives a colour that is compared with a chart to estimate pH. A calibrated pH probe gives a numerical reading. Compare readings taken under suitable, consistent conditions and distinguish an observation from an explanation. Adding an appropriate alkali to an acid moves the mixture towards neutrality as neutralisation occurs. A lower pH means a more acidic solution, but pH alone does not tell you the volume of solution or the total amount of acid present.",
