      "title": "Explain global atmospheric circulation",
      "explanation": "Global atmospheric circulation explains why we have rain belts and deserts. At the equator, intense heating causes air to rise, cool and condense, creating heavy rainfall. The rising air moves toward the subtropics, where it sinks, producing dry zones. This sets up three cells in each hemisphere: Hadley near the equator, Ferrel in mid-latitudes, and Polar at high latitudes. The interactions drive prevailing winds: trade winds toward the equator, westerlies in mid-latitudes, and polar easterlies near the poles. The result is characteristic climate zones and seasonal shifts. Illustration: three circulating cells with arrows showing rising air at the equator and sinking air at ~30°, 60°, and the corresponding wind belts.",
