      "title": "Explain coastal and fluvial processes",
      "explanation": "Coastal processes involve the action of waves and currents: hydraulic action and attrition break rocks; abrasion wears them down; longshore drift moves material along the shore; deposition builds beaches and spits. Fluvial processes describe how rivers erode, transport and deposit sediment: vertical erosion dominates in the upper course, while lateral erosion and transportation in the middle and lower course widen the valley and form meanders and floodplains. Mechanisms depend on energy; sudden wave impact creates cracks, while rivers deposit when velocity falls. Misconception: waves always erode and rivers merely transport; in reality, both environments produce erosion and deposition, changing landforms over time.",
