      "title": "Explain how geology affects coastlines",
      "explanation": "Geology shapes coastlines by rock type, hardness, joints and bedding. Resistant rocks such as chalk or limestone form headlands where waves exploit weaknesses slowly, while softer rocks like clay erode quickly to create bays. The presence of joints, faults and the bedding angle affects where cracks open and collapse occurs; discordant coastlines expose alternating rock types, making headlands and bays, whereas concordant coastlines may produce long beaches behind a single resistant layer. Erosion interacts with sea level and climate; geology sets the potential, while energy decides the speed. Misconception: geology alone decides the coastline; processes and marine energy are equally important.",
