      "title": "Use inverse trigonometric functions to find missing angles",
      "explanation": "Inverse trig finds an angle when you know a ratio of sides. Decide which ratio you have: opp/hyp uses arcsin, adj/hyp uses arccos, and opp/adj uses arctan. Compute θ with the appropriate inverse. In a right triangle, the two non-right angles sum to 90°, so the angle found is typically the acute one; the other angle is 90° − θ. When using a calculator, ensure it’s in degrees and round to the required precision. A common pitfall is using the wrong inverse function or ignoring the potential to take the complementary angle in some contexts. Illustration: if opp = 4 and hyp = 5, θ = arcsin(4/5) ≈ 53.1°.",
