      "title": "Evaluate development strategies",
      "explanation": "Development strategies aim to reduce the gap between rich and poor areas through infrastructure, education, health, governance, trade and aid. The mechanism varies: better infrastructure lowers costs; education raises skills; health boosts labour participation; governance reduces corruption; trade expands markets. Yet strategies can fail if funding is unsustainable, poorly planned or imposed without local buy‑in; aid can create dependency; large projects may harm environments or displace communities. Effective approaches blend good governance, local involvement and long‑term funding, and must fit context and changing conditions. Evaluation should weigh sustainability, equity and potential side effects, not just short‑term growth. Illustration: three interlocking gears labelled education, infrastructure and governance drive growth.",
