      "title": "Explain the demographic transition model",
      "explanation": "The demographic transition model links birth and death rates to development. In Stage 1 both rates are high; in Stage 2 deaths fall as health improves while births stay high, causing rapid population growth. Stage 3 sees births fall as education, urban living and women’s rights expand; Stage 4 has low birth and death rates and a stable population. Some writers add Stage 5, where births may fall below deaths and population shrinks. The mechanism is better health, sanitation, schooling and rights, which change family size decisions. A common misconception is that every country follows the same fixed order; some stall or skip stages due to policy, conflict or culture. Illustration: a simple line graph showing birth and death curves over time.",
