      "title": "Explain why the UK economy moved from industry to services",
      "explanation": "In many high‑income economies, manufacturing declined as automation and global competition grew, while services expanded in finance, professional work, research and ICT as investment in skills and digital infrastructure rose. The transition can raise productivity and create high‑value jobs, but gains are uneven across regions; some places lose manufacturing jobs and see weaker service growth. A common misconception is that deindustrialisation means the whole economy shrank; instead, the structure shifts, with services taking a larger share even as manufacturing remains present. Town centres change: factories close while offices and tech hubs rise. Illustration: a town silhouette with factories fading into office towers.",
