      "title": "Interpret gradients",
      "explanation": "Interpret gradients means reading what the slope of a graph tells us about rate of change. For a straight-line graph the gradient is constant and equal to the rise over run between any two points. For curved graphs, the gradient varies; the gradient at a point is the slope of the tangent line there, which represents the rate of change at that exact moment. So on a distance–time graph, the gradient at a moment is the speed; on a velocity–time graph, it is the acceleration. Visual cues: positive gradient indicates increasing Y, negative indicates decrease; a zero gradient means a flat section. A common misconception is treating the gradient of a curve as the average rate over a long interval; read it at the point using a small Δx around it.",
