      "title": "Transform functions",
      "explanation": "Transformations move a graph into a new position or change its shape by applying rules to x or y. With y = f(x) the simplest moves are vertical translations (up and down) and horizontal translations (left and right). A vertical stretch or compression multiplies y by a factor; a horizontal stretch or compression uses f(kx) to compress or stretch along the x-axis. Reflections flip the graph: y = -f(x) mirrors in the x-axis, while y = f(-x) mirrors in the y-axis. It's helpful to apply transformations in order: first adjust the inner function (shapes) and then the outer (sizes). A common misconception is that shifts change the size of a graph; they do not, they move it.",
