      "explanation": "Non-right-angled triangles are solved with two main tools: the sine rule and the cosine rule. The sine rule relates a side to the sine of its opposite angle: a/sin A = b/sin B = c/sin C. It is most straightforward when you know two angles and a side (AAS/ASA) or two sides and a non-included angle (SSA). The SSA case can yield 0, 1 or 2 triangles, so you must check feasibility by drawing the figure or calculating a height. The cosine rule, a^2 = b^2 + c^2 − 2bc cos A, handles two sides and the included angle, or all three sides when you want an angle. When solving for an angle, use the inverse cosine and verify with the triangle sum. A common misconception is SSA always giving a single solution.",
