      "explanation": "A composite means applying the inner function first, then the outer: (f ∘ g)(x) = f(g(x)). The order matters; fg(x) is not the same as gf(x). To form the composite, substitute g(x) into f, and be mindful of the domain: the composite is defined only where g(x) lies in the domain of f. Illustration: with f(x) = x^2 and g(x) = x + 2, (f ∘ g)(x) = (x + 2)^2. If g(x) sometimes falls outside the domain of f, the composite is not defined there. This keeps the process clear and avoids domain errors.",
